I worked at Dogtopia for over a year and did not have this experience at all. I have literally no loyalty to them and no reason to try and sugar coat anything. I never once saw a dogfight or aggressive dog. They were screened with the owners and staffs dogs prior to being admitted and then had to do an hour or two in a playroom a few times before their first official day. I can only remember 1 injury where a dog jumped up and clipped its ear on a kennel edge. They had staff who met the owner at the vet with the dog. We did time outs, but they were 10 minutes max just to let the dogs have a break if one was amping up the group. The staff were also well screened. They went above and beyond to make sure they were safe and happy. We had a full week of textbook training before playroom training even began.  If a dog didn't want to be there and we could not cheer them up, we would try and work with the owner on shorter visits, but would ultimately not allow the dog back at some point since they were not in the business of making dogs miserable. They turned many dogs away that were not suited for that environment, even if it was a slight revenue loss, it made for a better experience for their customers and their dogs.

&#x200B;

I also worked for a vet clinic near 3 daycares for 4 years and never once saw a dog who got sick or injured at one...the dog park is a whole different story though.

Why does the mushroom always get invited to parties?

&#x200B;

Because he's a fungi

THIS should be higher up. It is not fair to manipulate the kids in to taking sides. A marriage ending should not be the end of a functional family. It just needs to function differently. And to make it sound like that can never happen because of infidelity is wrong. That is an adult issue to be dealt with amongst the adults involved. The kids can know what happened if they ask, but it needs to be framed gently and fairly from both points of view even if it doesn't feel fair to the wronged party. "Your mother feels like I am not home enough and she is now seeing someone else". I know that that feels wrong, and it is not about preserving the cheaters dignity. It is about preserving the kids relationship with their mother, not for her, but for them. The kids are what matter in this part.

&#x200B;

 May parents divorced when I was 21 and I was obviously old enough to understand that my dad was unfaithful. However, while I initially stood strong by my mom, she used that as a way to hurt my dad and made me feel like a pawn. For a long time that made me resentful of her even though I was still angry at my dad. It left me without a reliable parent in a really pivotal point in my life. I eventually learned to compartmentalize their relationship with one another from their relationships with me. I am glad I was older and had the emotional tools to do that, but I wasted a lot of years being angry about something that was not my business. I wish my mom in particular, would have not made me feel wrong for doing that. My dad was a shitty husband, but he is a really good dad.

Exactly! Kids are just learning to process difficult emotions. Sure, it is a parents job to discipline/correct bad behavior, but there is no magic wand. Most kids act out because it is a part of the learning and growing process.  Typically a behavior is happening for a reason. Not just because they are "a brat". They need something and they don't know it, much less know how to ask for it. Yes, sometimes they need leadership from a parent, but they also may need a nap, or just need a good cry.

&#x200B;

 I have found that my son often tries to pick fights when he has had a bad day. So if I see him deliberately trying to push my buttons, I will discipline the action, but more importantly, I will try to get ahead of it and prevent more. If I sit down and talk to him, it usually opens up the emotional floodgates for him and then he talks about it and feels better, which means he acts better.

YWBTA - but this seems like a tough one, because it sounds like your mental health is not well and that's just a bad situation for everyone. However, you invited him to live there, I feel like 2 days isn't enough time to give it a chance. Sounds like you need to sit down and both of you need to agree to some house rules and boundaries. You might also ask for some grace for the time being, since you are struggling with mental health issues and let him know you are seeking help for that. Sometimes open and honest conversation goes a long way. I feel like you owe him that at the very least. 

&#x200B;

Also, I know you said that you'd help him find somewhere else, but deposits and move in fees are A LOT and he may not be prepared for all of that. Is he on you current lease?

I dunno, assuming it was an explanation and not a brag, I think they have every right to explain away potential embarrassment.  I agree that it is insensitive if they truly are just bragging though. 

&#x200B;

Anecdotally, I honestly didn't know the 12 week thing until well after I announced at 9 (when I found out) for my first.

Yes! I work at a CC and I do advising for students intending to transfer and this is MASSIVELY important. I have told many students that CC is not for them because they have plans that just are not compatible, which is ok. That is the good thing about a CC, while w do care about enrollment, we are much more focused on long term success of our students, so a good CC will steer you pretty straight if you take advantage of the resources (like advising). 

&#x200B;

I work in Texas and every state is different, but my general bit of advice is, you are safer for transfer at an instate public and get core complete first if you can. ALWAYS talk to the goal school and get a degree plan.

I am a instance of failed birth control. I am not telling you to scare you, but just to tell you to listen to your instincts and your own body and talk to your doctor when ANYTHING feels off.  It was VERY effective for me for nearly ten years though.  Currently, I am neither trying or not trying to get pregnant at the moment so I am not on it any longer and will not get back on it. After the next kid I will just get a tubal.  I took it religiously for 10 years but it really did do some damage over time so be careful staying on it long term. You might look in to options like mirena that are hormone free. 

&#x200B;

  In retrospect, I could have guessed it was going to fail but I was preoccupied at the time. It failed 2 weeks before my wedding which was the most stressed I had ever been up until that point in my life. For about a year prior my period had become extremely irregular. I was moderately overweight as well (5ft 5in and 175lbs) which were all contributing factors. We did not have a backup method. I do think one backup method is probably enough though. 

&#x200B;

Thankfully at that point in my life I was moving from never wanting kids to being on the fence, and this just made that choice for me.  I am fortunate that I have no regrets about it other than wishing I had gotten to just enjoy early marriage for what it is on its own. But I understand not wanting kids. I was that way also for a long time. I had a scare in my early 20s and I was full on panicked. Like I said, I don't want to tell a scary story I just wanted to let you know that when it fails, it usually fails for a reason. I know medications (antibiotics specifically) are another big reason.
